# TypeScript Best Practices for 2024

## Introduction

TypeScript has evolved significantly over the years, and with the latest features in TypeScript 5.x, there are new best practices that can help you write cleaner, more maintainable code.

## Advanced Type Features

### 1. Template Literal Types

Use template literal types for more precise string manipulation:

```typescript
type HttpMethod = 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE';
type ApiEndpoint = `/api/${string}`;
type FullUrl = `https://api.example.com${ApiEndpoint}`;

// Usage
const url: FullUrl = 'https://api.example.com/api/users';
```

### 2. Conditional Types

Leverage conditional types for dynamic type inference:

```typescript
type NonNullable<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;
type DeepPartial<T> = {
  [P in keyof T]?: T[P] extends object ? DeepPartial<T[P]> : T[P];
};

// Usage
interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  profile: {
    avatar: string;
    bio: string;
  };
}

type PartialUser = DeepPartial<User>;
```

## Modern Patterns

### 1. Branded Types

Create type-safe identifiers:

```typescript
type UserId = string & { readonly brand: unique symbol };
type Email = string & { readonly brand: unique symbol };

const createUserId = (id: string): UserId => id as UserId;
const createEmail = (email: string): Email => email as Email;

// Usage
const userId = createUserId('123');
const email = createEmail('user@example.com');

// This will cause a type error
// const invalid: UserId = '123';
```

### 2. Discriminated Unions

Use discriminated unions for better type safety:

```typescript
type LoadingState = {
  status: 'loading';
};

type SuccessState = {
  status: 'success';
  data: User[];
};

type ErrorState = {
  status: 'error';
  error: string;
};

type ApiState = LoadingState | SuccessState | ErrorState;

// Usage
const handleApiState = (state: ApiState) => {
  switch (state.status) {
    case 'loading':
      return <Spinner />;
    case 'success':
      return <UserList users={state.data} />;
    case 'error':
      return <ErrorMessage message={state.error} />;
  }
};
```

## Error Handling

### 1. Result Pattern

Implement a Result type for better error handling:

```typescript
type Result<T, E = Error> = {
  success: true;
  data: T;
} | {
  success: false;
  error: E;
};

const fetchUser = async (id: string): Promise<Result<User, string>> => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!response.ok) {
      return { success: false, error: 'User not found' };
    }
    const user = await response.json();
    return { success: true, data: user };
  } catch (error) {
    return { success: false, error: 'Network error' };
  }
};

// Usage
const result = await fetchUser('123');
if (result.success) {
  console.log(result.data.name);
} else {
  console.error(result.error);
}
```

## Performance Optimization

### 1. const assertions

Use const assertions for immutable data:

```typescript
const colors = ['red', 'green', 'blue'] as const;
type Color = typeof colors[number];

// This is more precise than string[]
const color: Color = 'red'; // OK
const invalidColor: Color = 'yellow'; // Error
```

### 2. satisfies operator

Use the satisfies operator for type checking without widening:

```typescript
const config = {
  apiUrl: 'https://api.example.com',
  timeout: 5000,
  retries: 3,
} satisfies Config;

// Type is preserved, not widened to Config
```

## Testing with TypeScript

### 1. Type-Safe Testing

Create type-safe test utilities:

```typescript
type TestUser = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
};

const createTestUser = (overrides: Partial<TestUser> = {}): TestUser => ({
  id: 'test-id',
  name: 'Test User',
  email: 'test@example.com',
  ...overrides,
});

// Usage in tests
test('should create user', () => {
  const user = createTestUser({ name: 'John Doe' });
  expect(user.name).toBe('John Doe');
});
```

## Conclusion

TypeScript continues to evolve with powerful new features. By adopting these best practices, you can write more robust, maintainable, and type-safe code.

Key takeaways:
- Use advanced type features like template literals and conditional types
- Implement modern patterns like branded types and discriminated unions
- Adopt the Result pattern for better error handling
- Leverage const assertions and satisfies operator for performance
- Write type-safe tests

## References

1. [TypeScript Official Documentation](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/)
2. [TypeScript 5.0 Release Notes](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-5-0/)
3. [TypeScript Handbook](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/intro.html)

> **Remember**: TypeScript is a tool to help you write better code. Use it to catch errors at compile time, not to make your code more complex.

---

